                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[b]Strania: The Stella Machina[/b] is made by people who have games like [b]G-Darius[/b] and [b]Ikaruga[/b] among their past credits, a \"pedigree\" that it shows.\n\nIt looks fantastic, save for a few slightly plasticky textures, despite being locked at 720p resolution. Right from the player's robot introducing itself with a close-up, the quality of the mecha design can be appreciated, with the opposing faction having some extremely stylized units (the first screenshot shows the first boss fight). There are plenty of visual effects, including some of the most satisfying explosions you'll see in a video game, but they're never overused; among the small details, I like the sparks that form where your bullets hit. There are many beautiful setpieces, like the second level starting with an assault in an enclosed space and ending with one of the coolest boss introductions ever (also featured in a store page screenshot): an archrival who fights on increasingly large red robots.\n\nThose setpieces aren't just for visual spectacle but are also functional to the level design and gameplay, with an alternation of classic high-speed vertically scrolling action and slower sections where you have to navigate between obstacles, with collisions being luckily more forgiving than \"one touch and you explode\"; there is even a part where the screen scrolls mostly horizontally, and a few others where the space to move around is much larger in that direction than vertically. The levels are so varied that the campaign feels much longer than the average playtime of 25 minutes for a full run.\n\nThere is a second campaign (originally a DLC but included from the get-go here), played with the Vower faction, that basically doubles the game. While of course produced using a lot of recycled assets, with several of the setpieces now played from the other side (the assault mission becomes a defense one and vice versa), it offers a lot of new content, including enemy waves, bosses, and of course confrontations with the robot you pilot in the original campaign, with even a better final clash. The clothing of the two Vower pilots is so ridiculously overdesigned, though.\n\nThe weapon system, based on picking up weapons according to the sides of the robot, is an acquired taste. I sometimes felt that, in the most frenetic sections, there was a strong risk of mistakenly picking one that was disadvantageous for the situation at hand. If used well, however, it's quite satisfying to pick weapons based not on power but on efficiency. Never sleep on swords, as they are often the best option in tight spaces (and they are the reason some have compared the game to Jaleco's [b]Cybattler[/b]).\n\n[b]Strania[/b] is difficult, very much so, and offers no options to make itself more accessible in that regard. The \"Normal\" difficulty is already stiff, and the true final battle is locked behind the next two difficulty levels. On that note, the timing and use of the weapon system to get the sword after a frankly annoying fight, where you're ridiculously disadvantaged compared to your opponent, can ruin the whole experience after all the effort it took just to get there in the first place. The final battle of the other campaign is much better, with both opponents going full power. Reportedly, the more recent EX version has fixed that issue, but there are no signs that it will ever be available on PC.\n\nI had serious issues with the sound. The game may play only the music or the sound effects, forcing you to avoid using sound peripherals to prevent this; it may also go mute, leaving no option but to quit and reload after adjusting their volume. And even then, the mixing didn't feel great. It's apparently an issue that has started showing up for users in more recent years (the game was updated shortly after the original release, and never again since).\n\nThe full potential of the game will be available only to a subset of players, which may have limited its appeal, while it remains [i]severely[/i] underrated (60 Steam reviews in over a decade, seriously?). But with G.rev having also reduced the base price in late 2024, it's still well worth owning one of the finest shmups of the last 15 years. Now, if only someone would port Border Down to PC...",
